WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.140
I wanted to be able to have my avatar
manifest some of those feelings

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.580
that I was actually experiencing.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.060
My name is Guillermo Bernal. I'm a graduate student

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:18.860
at the Media Lab at the Fluid Interfaces
Group. My research focus falls in the

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:22.080
back and forth between digital and
physical.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.440
Looking into how physiological

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.680
information like brain signals
electrodermal activity and any type of

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:33.989
physiological sensor can be used to
allow us to express in the digital world

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:36.300
our needs that we have.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.900
One thing that we did here is flexible pcbs that actually

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.980
begin to pick up physiological data from
EEG brain signals as well as like your

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.771
vocal cords and muscle explanations.

00:00:49.771 --> 00:00:52.949
And then there are some fiber optics that

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:59.039
are in here and when you have some sort
of activity it has an LED and begins to

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:03.989
brighten and have different patterns
almost replicating how like neural

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:08.620
networks begin to fire different microvolts in our brains.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.760
I think what inspires me is a mix of

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:18.180
the unknown and the excitement of something new.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.280
How can we make a technology more accessible?

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.780
How do we actually provide the means to show

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:29.960
how we feel? And how we want to
express in these virtual reality applications?

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:33.180
This project couldn't
happen without the help and assistance

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.850
from the Council for the Arts at MIT and
the MIT Media Lab summer program for

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:42.320
Hacking Manufacturing.

